WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.310 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  one month ago today a tornado ripped  through mayfield kentucky and devastated  the community there cleanup efforts are  still underway after a tornado that  wiped out neighborhoods destroyed iconic  sites and claimed the lives of nearly 80  people in the state of kentucky alone  novelist and pulitzer prize finalist  bobby ann mason grew up in mayfield and  in a special essay for cbs morning she  reflects on the catastrophe that forever  changed the place that she calls home  i grew up right here  on this farm in mayfield kentucky when i  was a kid i sat under the big trees and  read nancy drew mysteries  after college i moved away but i  returned often to see my parents who  stayed here in the house where i was  born  mayfield was a traditional little  southern town with a neat courthouse but  that's history  [Music]  just after thanksgiving  mayfield had a christmas parade around  the square  a celebration  unprecedented in its joy and civic pride  it's difficult to fathom how much pain  there is too among many of these  families  then  the tornado that roared through kentucky  a month ago  wrecked the center of town and toppled  the steeple of that courthouse and  demolished most of the buildings around  it it was merciless unrelenting and  indiscriminate in its power it was  shocking  most famously the tornado flattened the  candle factory  i closed my eyes and all i see is people  hollering and screaming for help  the devastation is worse than i imagined  when i saw it on tv  everything was just falling on top of us  i was under there somewhere  i'd never seen destruction bigger than  one house but this is blocks and blocks  and it goes from miles into the country  whole neighborhoods lost their homes and  jobs people were thrust out into the  unknown at christmas  some could go to relatives but many were  scattered at hotels and state park  lodges  the tornado was an enormous trauma  mayfield will be feeling the effects for  generations  i grieve for the people here  many blocks are small  modest houses  rentals are scarce and i don't know what  these residents will do  the large downtown churches were gutted  but they've made do in temporary homes  a big christmas tree rose triumphantly  in front of the wrecked courthouse  so there's hope  western kentuckians are resilient that's  no surprise  given the farm heritage of this region  [Music]  sometimes a crop fails  or a tornado blows your house away  nature can be cruel mayfield got a  terrible thrashing but people here are  strong  and they are busy making plans and  talking about what they want their town  to become  houses are already going up  mayfield faces so many questions  can we restore the courthouse  should we try to recreate the past or  think boldly about the future  challenges like technology politics and  climate  swirl around the whole world these days  with all this confusion how can we make  good choices  i hope we think hard  about what sort of new town we want to  make the tornado reminded us that  nothing is  permanent nothing is secure but starting  over from scratch is a great opportunity  to do something just brilliant  and speaking of brilliant that's  a novelist from mayfield in front of her  childhood home which was also destroyed  in that tornado uh a reminder that  people will bounce back and they're  asking themselves what kind of town do  we want to be now yeah talk about  putting a positive positive spin if you  can put a positive spin on that give us  an opportunity to do something brilliant  i love that i love that we have to  remember from the outside looking in  there's no expiration date on pain and  devastation that's right so they still  need help said it best nature can be  very cruel

